---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Buying Your First Skateboard"
  description="What makes a good starter skateboard setup, how to size one for your kid, and what to avoid."
>
  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Parent Resources &rsaquo; Buying Your First Skateboard</p>
      <h1>Save yourself a trip back to the store.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The single biggest mistake parents make is buying a "toy" board from
        a big-box store. Here's how to get it right the first time.
      </p>

      <h2>What to avoid</h2>
      <p>
        Boards sold at general toy or department stores are often built with
        low-grade plastic decks, soft wheels, and loose, poorly-made trucks
        (the metal axle assemblies). They're heavier, harder to control, and
        genuinely more frustrating to learn on &mdash; which can turn a kid
        off skateboarding before they've really tried it.
      </p>

      <h2>What a good starter setup looks like</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A "complete" from a skate shop</strong> &mdash; a
          pre-assembled board built from real skate components (7-ply maple
          deck, quality trucks and bearings). Most local skate shops sell
          beginner-friendly completes in the $80&ndash;$130 range.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Medium-hard wheels</strong> (roughly 95a&ndash;99a
          durometer) &mdash; soft enough to roll smoothly over small cracks,
          hard enough to still allow tricks later.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A deck width sized to the rider</strong>, not just their
          age &mdash; see sizing notes below.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Sizing by age (rough guide)</h2>
      <table class="size-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Age</th>
            <th>Typical deck width</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>5&ndash;7</td><td>7.0&Prime;&ndash;7.375&Prime;</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8&ndash;10</td><td>7.375&Prime;&ndash;7.625&Prime;</td></tr>
          <tr><td>11+</td><td>7.625&Prime;&ndash;8.0&Prime;+ (approaching adult sizing)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        When in doubt, size to the width of the rider's foot rather than
        their age &mdash; a narrower board is easier to control for smaller
        feet.
      </p>

      <h2>Budget guidance</h2>
      <p>
        A solid starter complete usually runs $80&ndash;$130. Add a helmet
        ($25&ndash;$45) and pads if you don't already have some from other
        sports. You don't need to spend more than that to get a setup that
        will genuinely work well while your kid is learning.
      </p>

      <p>
        Not ready to buy yet? <a href="/programs/free-clinics">Come try it at a free clinic</a>
        first &mdash; boards are provided.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .size-table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .size-table th,
  .size-table td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.5rem 1.25rem 0.5rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
</style>
